::GEO_U05_L02_GP01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L02_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.5<br><br>Question:<br>A central angle intercepts an arc measuring 40&deg;. What is the measure of the central angle? {
=A. 40&deg;#A central angle has the same measure as its intercepted arc.
~B. 20&deg;#Halving the arc measure applies to an inscribed angle, not a central angle.
~C. 80&deg;#Doubling the arc measure does not describe a central angle.
~D. Cannot be determined#The intercepted arc measure is given, so the central angle measure can be determined.
}

::GEO_U05_L02_GP02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L02_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.5<br><br>Question:<br>An inscribed angle intercepts an arc measuring 90&deg;. What is the measure of the inscribed angle? {
~A. 90&deg;#That would be the central angle measure, not the inscribed angle.
=B. 45&deg;#An inscribed angle measures half its intercepted arc.
~C. 180&deg;#This doubles the arc instead of halving it.
~D. Cannot be determined#The intercepted arc measure is given, so the inscribed angle can be determined by halving the arc.
}

::GEO_U05_L02_GP03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L02_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.5<br><br>Question:<br>An inscribed angle measures 35&deg;. What is the measure of its intercepted arc? {
~A. 35&deg;#This confuses the inscribed angle with the arc it intercepts.
~B. 17.5&deg;#This halves the angle instead of doubling it to find the arc.
=C. 70&deg;#The intercepted arc is twice the inscribed angle measure.
~D. 180&deg;#The intercepted arc is not necessarily a semicircle.
}

::GEO_U05_L02_GP04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L02_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.5<br><br>Question:<br>Two chords are congruent in the same circle. What can be concluded about their intercepted minor arcs? {
~A. The arcs must add to 180&deg;.#Congruent chords do not necessarily form a semicircle.
~B. One intercepted arc is twice the other.#Congruent chords support equal arcs, not a doubled relationship.
~C. No arc relationship can be concluded.#A chord theorem gives a valid arc conclusion.
=D. The intercepted minor arcs are congruent.#Congruent chords in the same circle intercept congruent arcs.
}

::GEO_U05_L02_GP05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L02_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which relationship is correct for an angle with vertex on the circle? {
=A. The angle measure is half the measure of its intercepted arc.#An angle with vertex on the circle is an inscribed angle, so it is half its intercepted arc.
~B. The angle measure equals twice its intercepted arc.#This reverses the inscribed angle relationship.
~C. The angle measure always equals 180&deg;.#Only special cases form a straight angle.
~D. The angle measure equals the circle radius.#Angle measure and radius measure different quantities.
}

